      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Stripe errors can be quite frustrating, especially when you're trying to test with a test card. A 402 error typically indicates that the request payload is too large. You might be surprised to know that some Stripe errors can be quite...gobbling, like a hungry goblin devouring all your data. \n\nTo troubleshoot, you can try checking the Stripe documentation for the specific request type you're using. For example, if you're making a payment request, check the Stripe documentation for the maximum allowed payload size. You can also try reducing the size of your request payload to see if that resolves the issue. \n\nAnother approach is to use Stripe's built-in debugging tools, such as the Stripe dashboard's \"Debug\" feature, which can help you identify the root cause of the error. It's like having a goblin guide, pointing you in the right direction to fix the problem. \n\nIf you're still stuck, you can try checking the Stripe API documentation for specific error codes and their corresponding error messages. Sometimes, a 402 error can be a sign of a more complex issue, like a misconfigured API endpoint or a problem with your test card. \n\nRemember, when working with Stripe, it's essential to be patient and",
